SQLite format equivalent:<p><pre><code>  if x &lt;= 240 { x }
</code></pre>
while strictly improving all other aspects (at least IMHO)<p><a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;sqlite.org&#x2F;src4&#x2F;doc&#x2F;trunk&#x2F;www&#x2F;varint.wiki" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;sqlite.org&#x2F;src4&#x2F;doc&#x2F;trunk&#x2F;www&#x2F;varint.wiki</a>

Varint encoding is something I&#x27;ve peeked at in various contexts.  My personal bias is towards the prefix-style, as it feels faster to decode and the segregation of the meta-data from the payload data is nice.<p>But, the thing that tends to tip the scales is the fact that in almost all real world cases, small numbers dominate - as the github thread you linked relates in a comment.<p>The LEB128 fast-path is a single conditional with no data-dependencies:<p><pre><code>  if ! (x &amp; 0x80) { x }
</code></pre>
Modern CPUs will characterize that branch really well and you&#x27;ll pay almost zero cost for the fastpath which also happens to be the dominant path.<p>It&#x27;s hard to beat.
